  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain chances continue through tonight, especially \nNE. However, rain could make it to the I-95 Corridor and Hampton \nRoads this evening. Significant rainfall is not expected. Except for \nlingering light rain over the eastern shore on Sunday, dry weather \nis expected next week. Temperatures will warm above average with mid \nto upper 80s forecast by late week.\n\nThe impressive Nor'easter continues to strengthen offshore, and the \ncenter is well defined on visible satellite. Rather pleasant and \nconditions prevail along and west of I-95, with lower-mid 70s, \npartly to mostly sunny skies, and low humidity. Temps are in the 60s \nin the coast with gusty winds and cloudy skies. Rain has \nunderperformed so far today, with only spotty light rain on the \nnorthern neck and eastern shore. The Wind Advisory has been dropped, \nbut gusty winds to 35-40 mph are possible on the eastern shore \nthrough this evening (though the greatest impacts by far from here \non out will be coastal flooding). A shortwave is progged to rotate \naround the back side of the low, tracking from NNW-SSE over eastern \nportions of the area this evening-tonight. This will allow steadier \nlight to moderate rain to move back in from the north. The highest \nrain chances are from 5 PM-1 AM on the eastern shore and northern \nneck. However, the rain could make it as far south and west as the \nnorthern I-95 Corridor, RIC Metro, and Hampton Roads after sunset \nbefore rain chances drop off later tonight after the shortwave \npasses by. Additional rain amounts likely will be in the 0.25-0.75\" \nrange on the eastern shore, with 0.10\" at best toward the I-95 \nCorridor. \n\nWith the low tracking to the NE on Sunday and low-level flow turning \nto the WNW (gusts to 30 mph possible during the day), expect partly \ncloudy skies/dry wx for most of the area with highs in the 70s. \nHowever, clouds and light rain will linger on the eastern shore with \nhighs likely not making it out of the 60s. The low weakens and will \nbe far enough away from the area on Monday for another day in the \n70s...with 70F readings even possible on the eastern shore where at \nleast some sun is expected.\n\nHigh pressure moves over the area on Tuesday before pushing offshore \non Wed as upper ridging builds over the E/SE CONUS. This will lead \nto a warming trend with mainly dry wx for the rest of the week. \nAbove average temperatures in the mid to upper 80s are possible by \nThu/Fri.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread moderate to locally major tidal flooding \n(inundation of 2-3+ feet AGL in vulnerable areas) continues tonight \ninto Sunday morning. Tides begin to subside Sunday at most spots, \nwith some locations especially in the middle Bay seeing flooding \npersist through the weekend.\n\nThe Nor'easter continues to develop off the DELMARVA coast\nthis afternoon. This has allowed N/NW winds to remain elevated,\nresulting in elevated tides. Tidal anomalies of 1-3 ft above \nnormal astronomical tide continue with at least one more round\nof widespread moderate to locally major coastal flooding\nexpected with this evening's high tide. Tidal anomalies \ndecrease Sunday as winds become NW. However, widespread minor to\nmoderate coastal flooding is still expected Sunday morning. \nLocally major coastal flooding is possible Sunday, mainly across\nthe middle Ches Bay along the Eastern Shore as the NW winds \npush water towards the eastern side of the bay. Conditions \nfinally begin to notably improve Sun evening into Mon as winds \ndiminish. Nevertheless, additional rounds of nuisance to minor \ncoastal flooding will continue to be possible through Mon. \nCoastal Flood Warnings remain in effect through this evening's \nhigh tide. However, additional extensions are likely, especially\nacross the middle Ches Bay.",
